## Analysis: Date-Boundary Flakiness in Billing Tests

Three findings converge to explain the flakiness:

### Most Likely Cause: `billing.ts:92` — Temporal Recklessness (crime_00018)

```ts
const daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));
```

This drives the loyalty discount tiers (90/180/365 days). Because it calls `Date.now()` directly, a test that sets `user.createdAt` to "90 days ago" will see `daysSince` flip between **89** and **90** depending on which side of midnight the test runs — toggling the discount between 0% and 2%, which changes `discountCents` and `totalCents`.

The same pattern repeats at `billing.ts:273` in `recommendUpgrade`.

### Contributing factors

| Finding | File | Impact |
|---------|------|--------|
| crime_00018 | `billing.ts` | 7 bare `Date.now()`/`new Date()` calls — tests can't pin time |
| crime_00010 | `date.ts:52` | `+ 86400000` ms day math is wrong on DST transitions |
| crime_00003 | `date.ts:41` | Mixes `getUTCFullYear()` with local `getMonth()` — wrong date near midnight |
| crime_00019 | `billing.test.ts:4-6` | "renders the enterprise plan" has **zero assertions** — gives false confidence |
| crime_00037 | `billing.test.ts:8-10` | "creates an invoice" only checks `toBeTruthy()` on a stub — never exercises `generateInvoice` at all |

### Proposed Fix

**Inject a clock into `generateInvoice` and `recommendUpgrade`** so tests can pin time:

```ts
// billing.ts
export function generateInvoice(
  user: User,
  items: InvoiceLine[],
  paymentMethod?: string,
  now: () => number = Date.now,  // injectable clock
): Invoice {
  const createdAt = now();
  // ...
  const daysSince = Math.floor((now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));
  // ...
}
```

```ts
// billing.test.ts — pinned clock eliminates boundary flakiness
it("applies 2% loyalty discount after exactly 90 days", () => {
  const createdAt = Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1);          // Jan 1 2026
  const now = () => Date.UTC(2026, 3, 1);           // Apr 1 2026 (90 days)
  const invoice = generateInvoice(user, items, undefined, now);
  expect(invoice.discountCents).toBe(/* expected */);
});
```

The clock injection is the minimal fix — it makes every `daysSince` threshold deterministic without requiring a date library or monkey-patching globals. The existing tests should then be rewritten to actually exercise `generateInvoice` with real assertions at each discount boundary.
